WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:07.129
OK, it is time for a weekly coronavirus update,
I think weekly is sort of the right timing

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:08.129
to do.

00:00:08.129 --> 00:00:12.980
These were now not really following day to
day data, but more looking at things week

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:17.189
over week and looking at trends and changes
to cases and deaths and vaccinations.

00:00:17.189 --> 00:00:18.800
So let's jump right in.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.689
Things are still looking continually better.

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:23.779
They're looking better than last week on average.

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:28.070
But there's now sort of a word of caution
about some of the last few days of data.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.980
And again, because we're looking week over
week, I don't think we should read too much

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:31.980
into it now.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.140
But I'll explain momentarily.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:41.739
From the standpoint of coronavirus cases,
cases are now down 72 percent from their average

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:44.960
peak, which took place on January 11th.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.250
On January 11th are seven day average was
two hundred and fifty five thousand new cases

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.740
per day.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:01:00.649
When we think back to June of twenty twenty,
when Dr. Fauci said if we're not careful,

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:03.340
we might get to 100000 cases a day.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.080
And many, Denyer said that'll never happen.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.910
And we got to 100000 in 150 and 200 and we
were averaging two hundred and fifty five

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.580
thousand new cases a day.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.400
The week of January 11th.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:22.740
We are now down from that peak by 72 percent,
72 percent decline in new daily cases to about

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.790
70000 per day.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.890
But and here's that word of caution.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:34.520
The decline has leveled off in the last few
days and there was even a slight, average

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.490
day to day uptick yesterday.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.480
Now, a lot of people have been asking and
e-mailing me and tweeting to me, is this an

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.200
actual reversal or cases now going to go up?

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.200
I don't think so.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.980
I think it's possible that if indeed the vaccines
are shown to be ineffective against the primary

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.600
variants, that in a couple of months cases
could actually start going up in earnest.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.530
I don't think that that is going to be the
case, but that's not what we're talking about.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:04.320
Now, what is going on right now is that because
of the terrible weather in much of the country

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.539
last week, testing and reporting of cases
was slowed down.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:15.180
So most likely there were declines that were
reported late last week, which were bigger

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:21.140
than the real declines because weather stopped
a bunch of testing and reporting.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.260
And now as the reporting gets caught up and
people who should have been tested Friday,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.050
Saturday, Sunday, but weren't because of bad
weather are getting tested and are coming

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.290
back positive.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.140
And so the numbers are kind of leveling off
and appearing a bit higher.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.980
I don't think this is a real reversal.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.510
I think this is an artifact of the reporting
changes because of the weather.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.530
Now, in terms of deaths, the numbers have
also improved dramatically.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.710
Unfortunately, the numbers were so bad when
it comes to deaths that even these improved

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.310
numbers are still completely tragic.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:57.180
But deaths are down 38 percent from their
mid January highs.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.970
Deaths were peaked at 3400 per day.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.670
They're down to about 20, 100 per day now.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.280
Twenty one hundred per day is outrageous.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:12.090
It's an outrageous tragedy every single day,
but it is a decline of 38 percent and similar

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:17.980
to cases, a small uptick in the last couple
of days, also believed to be because of lower

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:24.370
reporting due to the bad weather late last
week, which is now recovering.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.569
And the data will sort of be shuffled around.

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:32.870
And I think we'll be back to kind of normal
day to day tracking within another 48 to 72

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:33.870
hours.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:39.520
We'll know in the next four to six days whether
there really is a reversal here, cases and

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.730
deaths leveling or even even going up a bit,
or whether this is just a reporting artifact

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.510
and cases and deaths will continue down.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:54.980
Now, much like case and death reporting was
slowed down because of the bad weather.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.900
So we're vaccinations slowed down.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:03.860
We had actually gotten up to averaging about
one point seven million shots per day.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:09.400
But because of the bad weather and important
to understand, the bad weather affected vaccinations

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.420
in the states with the bad weather like Texas,
for example.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:19.140
But the bad weather also affected shipments
of vaccines to more of the country.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:25.510
And last week, we saw vaccinations per day,
a drop from one point seven million a day

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:27.540
to one point four million a day.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:33.270
Now, that's still much higher from where we
were January 20th, for example.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.470
It's not high enough.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:40.720
And Dr. Fauci says he's confident that the
doses are going to be made up for in the next

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.180
week or so.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.180
Let's hope that happens.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.760
And obviously, we're following that number
very closely.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.320
The three numbers we're looking at closely
now are cases, deaths and vaccinations, hospitalizations.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:57.950
Also an important indicator to look at now,
vaccines in terms of vaccine news and important

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:59.060
studies.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:05.130
As I said yesterday in our segment about vaccine
alarmism, remember, vaccine alarmism is not

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:12.130
anti vax, vaccine alarmism is something else
we described yesterday, people just being

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:18.490
unnecessarily negative about the impact of
these vaccines, although the effect on transmission

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:24.030
by being vaccinated was not studied by Moderna
and Pfizer.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:28.930
We are getting more and more early data from
the United Kingdom and Israel, which gets

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:36.560
us closer and closer to being able to say
fully vaccinated people are transmitting,

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.870
are able to transmit coronavirus to a much
lesser degree.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:46.550
Two studies, one from the UK, says that two
doses of the Pfizer vaccine cut transmission

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.270
risk by 86 percent.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.020
Now, normal caveats.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.830
This is a preprint.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.790
This wasn't studied in the Phase three trials
by Moderna and Pfizer.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.290
So it is now being studied.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:02.780
Preprint UK data says transmission down 86
percent among fully vaccinated people.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:10.880
A study from Israel found an 89 percent reduction
in transmission among fully vaccinated people.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.190
This is good news, which still needs to be
confirmed in peer reviewed settings until

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:21.680
it is confirmed we should not be acting belligerently
once vaccinated.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:27.979
Now, as far as vaccine supply right now, and
this gets to one of the main questions, which

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:34.130
is when will person A, person B, person C
be able to get a vaccine?

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:35.669
It will vary state to state.

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:39.960
We have seen very uneven rollouts in some
states versus others.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:46.240
As far as vaccine supply, it looks like the
United States will have enough vaccine for

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.740
full vaccination of one hundred and thirty
million people by the end of March.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:53.370
Now, that's five weeks away.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.710
But remember that it takes up to five weeks
to be fully vaccinated.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:03.229
What I mean by that is with the Phizer vaccine,
you have 21 days between dose one and two

00:07:03.229 --> 00:07:07.979
with the Moderna vaccine, it's 28 days between
dose one and two.

00:07:07.979 --> 00:07:12.400
And you're not considered fully protected
until seven days after your second dose.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.300
So if you get the Moderna vaccine, if you
can get it by March 31st, then let's imagine

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:23.960
you get an appointment, you get your second
dose on April 28th, and that means you are

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.860
not considered fully protected until roughly
May 5th.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:29.860
That would be OK.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.270
So it's about a 35 day period from when you
get it until when you're considered fully

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:35.320
vaccinated.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.680
However, there is more good news, and that
is that the third vaccine, the Johnson and

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.800
Johnson vaccine, is on the way today.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.860
It has been endorsed for emergency use authorization
by FDA staff.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.740
It now needs to go through the kind of final
final process for emergency use authorization.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.020
That is a one dose vaccine.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:01.699
The good news is one dose makes logistics
far simpler and X number of doses is good

00:08:01.699 --> 00:08:03.610
for twice as many vaccinations.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:04.610
Right.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:07.389
If you have ten million doses, but everybody
needs to you can vaccinate five million people.

00:08:07.389 --> 00:08:12.580
If you have ten million doses and people only
need one, you can vaccinate ten million people.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:19.080
The cautious possible downside on the JNJ
vaccine supply is unclear.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.900
And I think we'll just leave it there because
unclear is basically tells you everything

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:24.900
we need to know.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.360
We just don't know yet how much supply of
that will be available, how quickly.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:35.029
So I think where the facts lead us is to continue
to cautious optimism as far as variants go

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:41.479
will be interviewing Dr. Eric Topol again
soon about the effectiveness of these vaccines

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:43.809
against the three most prevalent variants.

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:45.350
That's a topic of concern right now.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:09:53.899
It's looking OK and we'll have more information
about that soon.

00:09:53.899 --> 00:10:03.189
And in our interview with Dr. Eric Topol.

